  "title": "Hotels, tourism and short-term rentals",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "portuguese-for-work run, 2026-11-22: written by hand for Portugal (European Portuguese, pt-PT), not yet read by a human. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon, the IMT deck or another sector are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions, contracts and rules are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Tourism is one of Portugal's biggest employers: hotels and guesthouses, holiday flats (alojamento local), and seasonal work in the Algarve, Lisbon, Porto and Madeira. This deck has reception and the rooms, the tasks, bookings and check-in, safety and cleaning, and talking to guests and the team. Guests get the third person, o senhor / a senhora or simply the verb: «Tem reserva?» Many guests speak English, but a Portuguese greeting always goes down well.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in a hotel or with holiday flats in Portugal? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Empregado/a de andares",
    "Rececionista",
    "Governanta",
    "Empregado/a de limpeza (alojamento local)",
    "Bagageiro",
    "Animador/a turístico/a"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "At reception, start with «Bom dia, bem-vindo» (bem-vinda to a woman) and the guest's name if you know it. Then «Tem reserva?» and «Posso ver o seu documento?»",
    "In housekeeping, knock and say «Limpeza!» before you go in, and never enter a room with the «Não incomodar» sign.",
    "Tourism work is often seasonal (a época alta), and many contracts are fixed-term (a termo). Ask for your contract in writing and keep your payslips. General orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
